from research_schema import V

V('camille-monet-in-japanese-costume-la-japonaise','The holding museum dates La Japonaise to 1876, not the lecture’s 1875. It identifies oil on canvas and Camille wearing a blond wig. Retain 1875 only as the original lecture assertion.','mfa-japonaise','correction',resolves=['Slide date requires museum verification.'])
V('under-mannen-bridge-at-fukagawa','The Met dates the Hokusai design to ca.1830–32 and places it in Thirty-six Views of Mount Fuji; the lecture’s 1823 is inconsistent with that catalogue. This verifies the design, not ownership of the particular photographed impression.','met-mannen','correction',resolves=['1823 slide date needs verification.'])
V('antiquities-of-india-title-page','The British Library’s title-sheet record gives 15 October 1799 and etching with aquatint. This supports the visible imprint and corrects a generic engraving label. The lecture separately gives 1808; see the Attribution Note for that bibliographic question.','bl-daniell','partial_correction')
V('the-letter-mary-cassatt','The NGA records the final colour print as softground etching, drypoint and aquatint. The Met’s separate first-state record is drypoint; do not transfer that state-specific label to the finished colour impression.','nga-cassatt met-cassatt-state')
V('kali-and-tara-calcutta-art-studio','The Met identifies this diptych as an 1885–90 chromolithograph by Calcutta Art Studio, object 2018.292. This verifies the colour-print process.','met-kali-tara')
V('self-portrait-of-farrukh-beg-lecture-identification','Wonder of the Age, catalogue 24, p.67, identifies a presumed self-portrait, ca.1615, in the Seitz collection. Preserve presumed: the self-identification is qualified, and publication by the Met does not mean the painting belongs to the Met.','met-farrukh','qualification')
V('shah-jahan-on-a-terrace-holding-a-portrait-pendant','The Met records Chitarman’s recto as 1627–28, folio from the Shah Jahan Album, accession 55.121.10.24. The catalogue identifies European angel imagery and the representation of several courtly crafts. Recto painting and album assembly are distinct.','met-shahjahan')
V('a-goldsmith-in-his-shop','The Met treats the mirror’s moral reading as a possibility and explains that a later-added halo was removed. The shopkeeper should not therefore be asserted as Saint Eligius on the basis of that halo.','met-goldsmith','qualification')
V('diospyros-melanoxylon-roxburgh-botanical-plate','RBGE distinguishes Roxburgh as commissioner from the Indian artists who made drawings. This corrects the common attribution shortcut; the individual makers of this specific printed plate remain unnamed here.','rbge-roxburgh','qualification')
V('veni-vidi-vici-l-n-tallur','The Biennale’s catalogue gives 2012–13, terracotta roof tiles and iron, at Aspinwall House. The lecture’s 2012 refers to the installation’s opening context. A later museum account supports the material’s mission-industry history; it is not used to substitute a later version’s date.','tallur-biennale tallur-linden')
V('jahangir-shooting-the-head-of-malik-anbar-on-a-cosmic-globe','The Smithsonian identifies the allegory and its bull-and-fish support. The owl study explains its hostile imagery and imagined victory over Malik Ambar. The lecture spelling Anbar is retained as an alias; it does not identify a different person.','si-malik owl-study')
